<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Detailed comparison of the two enterprise use cases evaluated in our benchmark.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation of agentic AI systems has emerged as a critical research area, with existing benchmarks examining various components of agent architectures. However, these efforts have predominantly focused on isolated aspects of agentic systems rather than their integrated performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool calling</span> is perhaps the most fundamental aspect of all agentic systems. Existing tool calling benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. 2024</a>)</cite> have revealed significant LLM weaknesses in key aspects of tool calling including tool selection accuracy, tool input accuracy, valid output formatting, and issues with latency and unnecessary repetition of tool calls. Additional benchmarks focus on more complex scenarios finding that LLM performance on tool calling decreases when combined with complex instruction following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. 2025</a>)</cite> and multi step reasoning in nested tool calling scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Basu et al. 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another key aspect of agentic systems is <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">memory and context management</span>. LLMs are inherently limited by their context windows which provides a unique challenge for those building agentic systems to ensure that the LLMs keep the most relevant information in context. This is relevant for both conversational and tool-use settings, as conversational context accumulates over time, while tool calls can significantly increase context length due to their frequency or output size <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maharana et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>. Existing research presents a variety of mechanisms for improving memory management in agentic systems such as treating agent memory as a self directed operating system with different storage tiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Packer et al. 2024</a>)</cite> , creating dynamic knowledge networks for connecting information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, and implementing a dual-track memory system meant to resemble long-term and short-term memory in the human brain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As LLMs enable more complex agentic workflows, <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Agent Systems (MAS)</span> have emerged to support use cases involving specialized agents, prompting the development of benchmarks to evaluate their effectiveness. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. 2025</a>)</cite> examine LLM performance across various MAS structures, including hierarchical and distributed planning, evaluating task completion and planning capabilities to assess LLM effectiveness in both planning and execution roles. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agashe, Fan, and Wang 2025</a>)</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng and Chang 2025</a>)</cite> focus specifically on evaluating planning and coordination between agents in MAS finding that planning abilities are a key component of success in MAS. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al. 2025</a>)</cite> illustrate how multi-agent systems can benefit from routing frameworks that help ensure that the appropriate agents, communication strategies, and LLMs are selected for a given task. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou, Tang, and
Wang 2025</a>)</cite> introduce a dynamic MAS framework that incorporates hierarchical planning along with a search process for selecting the optimal multi-agent configuration for the task at hand. Other work on dynamically generated MAS demonstrates how scaling single agents into MAS can lead to performance increases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Recent work compares heterogeneous LLMs versus a single model in MAS, highlighting how MAS can be used to leverage individual model strengths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As our work is primarily focused on <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">enterprise benchmarks</span>, we also explore existing work on agents in enterprise domains. Enterprise-focused evaluation has consistently revealed a gap between existing academic benchmarks and real-world applicability, with even state-of-the-art models struggling on simple workplace tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Styles et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Benchmarks across different enterprise domains consistently show that realistic business task complexity significantly exceeds what current models can handle reliably, with performance typically degrading in multi-turn interactions and interconnected tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Drouin et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Styles et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">While prior benchmarks have advanced understanding of individual agentic components, they remain fragmented—evaluating tool use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al. 2025</a>)</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Basu et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maharana et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>, and coordination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng and Chang 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agashe, Fan, and Wang 2025</a>)</cite> in isolation. Existing enterprise benchmarks address task complexity but lack systematic analysis of how architectural choices interact across scenarios. As a result, developers lack empirical guidance for selecting and combining agentic components under real-world enterprise constraints.</p>
</div>
