<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary evaluation metric is the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acceptable Score</span>, which measures the percentage of records that satisfy all three critical success conditions simultaneously:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Acceptable}=\frac{|\{r\in R:C(r)\land A(r)\land O(r)\}|}{|R|}\times 100\%" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Acceptable</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acceptable}=\frac{|\{r\in R:C(r)\land A(r)\land O(r)\}|}{|R|}\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all test records, and for each record <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(r)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates correct tool choice</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(r)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates correct tool arguments (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(r)=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(r)=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(r)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates correct final decision</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We define our main acceptability metric in this way because it comes closest to representing success in a real world agentic system. Simply arriving at the expected decision may be insufficient in enterprise deployments. If the model arrives at the right choice, that a leave request should be submitted example, but then does not call the tool to submit the request or notify the employee, the task is not complete. Furthermore, if the model selects all the correct tools and makes the correct final decision but uses the wrong input values for a tool, that could lead to adverse outcomes such as a request being made for the wrong employee or a database being updated with a hallucinated value. For this reason, we consider an agentic flow to be successful if and only if all the expected tools have been called with expected values and the correct final decision is made based on the results of the tool calls.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool Choice Evaluation</span>
We evaluate tool choice under two conditions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lenient Acceptable</span> (<math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{L}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{L}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>): The model executes all required ground truth tools but may perform additional read-only tools. Extraneous write operation tools are penalized.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Strict Acceptable</span> (<math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{S}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{S}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>): The model must execute exactly the ground truth tools in the correct order with no extraneous tools and zero hallucinations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool Arguments Scoring</span>
Tool arguments receive percent scoring where <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(r)=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(r)=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> only when all arguments are correct (compared to ground truth).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reliability Metrics</span>
To assess system reliability, we evaluate each configuration over <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math> attempts and compute <span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pass@1</span>—the percent of correct samples over k trials) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al. 2025</a>)</cite> and <span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pass</span>ˆ<span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">K</span>—the probability of <span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> k trials succeeding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. The latter metric is particularly important in real world enterprise domains where reliability and consistency is critical.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We use <span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acceptable pass@1</span> as our primary metric. We use lenient correct tool choice for the acceptable score as opposed to strict as strict scoring remains extremely challenging for most models. We use <span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pass@1</span> as it brings stability to the lenient acceptable metric over <span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">k</span> attempts, representing the success rate for a given model and configuration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additional Metrics</span>
We track supplementary behavioral metrics including:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hallucination rate</span>: Frequency of selecting non-existent agents or tools</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tool Repetition rate</span>: Frequency of consecutive identical tool calls with identical arguments</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Overall Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The benchmark results reveal that complex enterprise workflows remains a challenging task even for state-of-the-art language models in agentic systems. The simpler requesting time-off task (TO) showed had a peak score of 70.8% (GPT-4.1), while the complex customer routing task (CR) proved significantly more difficult, with scores peaking at only 35.3% (Sonnet 4). Only three models—GPT-4.1, Sonnet 4, and o3-mini—demonstrated meaningful capability on the complex customer request routing task, with most configurations achieving only single-digit scores. Performance varied dramatically across architectural configurations, indicating that no single architecture is optimal for all models. Furthermore, models achieved peak scores on different configurations between use cases suggesting that the optimal agentic configuration is in part dependent on the complexity of the task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With regards to architecture elements, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">thinking tools</span> consistently improved performance for non-reasoning models on the simpler time off task. GPT-4.1 improved from 48.5% to 70.8% with thinking tools enabled in single-agent function calling with summarized memory. However, thinking tools provided minimal benefit for o3-mini (55.8% to 56.7%) in its best performing category. Thinking tools showed minimal impact on the complex customer routing use case across all models. Compared to other elements, variation in <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">memory management</span> styles and
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">orchestration strategies</span> (within multi-agent architectures) both had minimal impact on scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function calling</span> generally outperformed <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ReAct</span> across most models. The notable exception was LLaMA 3.3 70B, which achieved its peak score of 12.2% using single-agent ReAct on the requesting time off task. <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-agent ReAct</span> consistently underperformed suggesting that ReAct may be better suited towards single-agent systems. <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucinations</span> for all models (except for GPT-4o) were found exclusively under ReAct settings suggesting that while models may be robust to hallucinations on the function calling schemas they have been trained on, they still have the propensity to hallucinate in ReAct settings. This is particularly relevant on the more complex use case in multi-agent systems. Sonnet 4, for example, showed hallucination rates of 36-36% on ReAct in multi-agent systems but 0% hallucination in every other configuration. See Table 4 for hallucination rates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While some models achieved their highest overall scores on single-agent architectures, the <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">correct final decision</span> scores for the complex customer routing use case were generally higher for multi-agent architectures. Although Sonnet 4’s highest overall scores were all achieved with single-agent function calling (all scores above 33%), its highest correct final decision scores were for function calling with multi-agent systems (84-87%) while for single agent it was in the 72-76% range. GPT-4.1 showed similar results as it achieved its peak overall score in both use cases with single agent function calling; however, on the customer routing task it achieved correct final decision rates of 97-99% with multi-agent function calling and only 79-86% with single agent function calling. GPT-4.1-mini also achieved significantly higher correct final decision rates with multi-agent function calling than with single-agent function calling on both use cases. This suggests that while some models are more likely to choose the correct tool or tool arguments on single agent systems, multi-agent systems may be more effective for selecting the correct final decision after all tool calls have been made. See Table 5 for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Two key patterns emerged from the <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">consistency analysis</span> shown in Table 3. Sonnet 4 and GPT-4.1 showed the most robust performance with relatively low coefficients of variation (32.1% and 27.0% respectively for the simpler task), making them the most reliable choices across different agentic configurations. Conversely, o3-mini exhibited extreme sensitivity to architectural choices (CV = 143.7%).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reliability Analysis</span>. Passˆk scores across all models and agentic configurations peak at 0.0634 indicating only a 6.34% chance of executing the workflow correctly in all 8 trials. In enterprise settings, where reliability and consistency are essential, pass@1 and passˆk results reveal a fundamental gap between the promise of agentic LLMs and their real-world performance. See appendix A.4 for full scores.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Model Specific Results</h3>

<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:505.9pt;height:172.8pt;vertical-align:-84.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-104.8pt,35.8pt) scale(0.707085236127795,0.707085236127795) ;">
